WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.839
Complex prompts need structural signals the model can rely on,

2
00:00:03.839 --> 00:00:07.599
and XML tags are the most reliable way to provide them.

3
00:00:07.599 --> 00:00:11.840
For this one, we'll look at why XML outperforms plain delimiters,

4
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.200
walk through the standard tag conventions, explore nested tags for hierarchical data,

5
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:23.600
and finish on the role structure plays in defending against prompt injection. Let's dive in.

6
00:00:24.559 --> 00:00:30.479
Imagine handing the model a wall of text where your instructions and a customer's email

7
00:00:30.479 --> 00:00:32.959
sit side-by-side with nothing between them.

8
00:00:33.599 --> 00:00:37.520
That ambiguity is exactly what XML tags remove.

9
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.400
XML tags are structured markup elements,

10
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.599
things like document, instructions, or example, that label and separate parts of a prompt.

11
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.599
Claude is specifically trained to respect those boundaries,

12
00:00:49.599 --> 00:00:52.639
which makes them more reliable than prose separators.

13
00:00:53.279 --> 00:00:57.520
Prompt injection is an attack where malicious user-supplied content

14
00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.919
tries to override the developer's instructions.

15
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.919
Tags help the model tell data from directives, but they aren't the primary defense.

16
00:01:05.919 --> 00:01:10.720
The real mitigations are treating tool and document content as untrusted

17
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.400
and giving the model least privilege.

18
00:01:12.959 --> 00:01:17.440
Tag consistency means using the same tag names for the same roles throughout,

19
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.160
which reinforces the structural signal Claude relies on when parsing your input.

20
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.040
Let's start with why XML beats plain delimiters.

21
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.839
When you use dashes, asterisks, or markdown headers as separators,

22
00:01:31.839 --> 00:01:36.239
the model has to infer whether they're structure signals or actual content.

23
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.839
That inference can go wrong, especially in longer prompts where section boundaries matter.

24
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.199
XML tags have unambiguous, open, and close syntax.

25
00:01:47.199 --> 00:01:51.120
There's no guessing whether a line of dashes is a separator or part of a table.

26
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.360
Claude's training reinforces tag boundaries consistently,

27
00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.400
so the model reliably treats the content inside a tag as a distinct section.

28
00:02:00.959 --> 00:02:05.440
For any prompt where you need clear, dependable separation between sections,

29
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.800
XML is the right choice over prose or markdown.

30
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.440
Four standard tag names cover most of what you'll build.

31
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.080
The instructions tag wraps the task directive, the thing you're asking the model to do.

32
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.880
The context tag holds background information the task depends on,

33
00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.479
things the model needs to know but isn't being asked to produce.

34
00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:32.240
The examples tag contains few-shot demonstrations of the output format or reasoning style you want.

35
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.600
The output format tag specifies the structure and format you expect back from the model.

36
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.559
You're not required to use these exact names, but they're widely recognized conventions.

37
00:02:42.559 --> 00:02:46.479
Using them makes your prompts easier for collaborators to read

38
00:02:46.479 --> 00:02:49.839
and maintains the kind of consistency that improves follow-through.

39
00:02:51.039 --> 00:02:54.720
Good tag naming is a small habit that pays off at scale.

40
00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.119
Choose names that describe what the content is, not just that it exists.

41
00:02:59.119 --> 00:03:03.440
A tag named customer data is clearer than data or block.

42
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.440
Stay consistent with those names.

43
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.520
If you call something document in one prompt, call it document everywhere,

44
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:13.199
because when tag names drift, the structural signal weakens.

45
00:03:13.839 --> 00:03:17.119
Every tag also needs its matching close.

46
00:03:17.119 --> 00:03:20.880
An unclosed tag is a common source of subtle bugs,

47
00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.039
where the model treats everything after the opening tag as part of that section.

48
00:03:25.039 --> 00:03:27.679
And if you are building a system with many prompts,

49
00:03:27.679 --> 00:03:30.479
align your tag vocabulary across all of them.

50
00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.720
That shared vocabulary is what keeps a multi-prompt system coherent and debuggable as it grows.

51
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.199
Flat and nested tags serve different needs.

52
00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:44.479
Flat tags work well when sections are independent.

53
00:03:44.479 --> 00:03:48.639
You have instructions, context, and examples at the same level,

54
00:03:48.639 --> 00:03:51.279
each clearly labeled, no hierarchy needed.

55
00:03:51.839 --> 00:03:54.479
That covers most single document prompts.

56
00:03:55.119 --> 00:03:59.440
Nested tags come into play when a section has its own internal structure.

57
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.800
If you're passing a document with a source, a date, and the actual content,

58
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.839
nesting a source tag and a content tag inside a parent document tag

59
00:04:09.839 --> 00:04:12.160
preserves those relationships.

60
00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.440
The model can read each child independently while knowing they belong to the same document.

61
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.279
Nesting adds clarity when hierarchy exists.

62
00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:23.519
It adds noise when it doesn't.

63
00:04:23.519 --> 00:04:26.720
So keep it to one or two levels in practice.

64
00:04:28.079 --> 00:04:32.320
Nested tags let you represent structured data without ambiguity.

65
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.760
A document tag can contain a source tag, a date tag, and a document content tag as children.

66
00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.480
The model reads each child independently within the context of its parent.

67
00:04:43.119 --> 00:04:47.200
That parent-child relationship matters when you have multiple documents.

68
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.320
Each one has its own source and content, and the tags make those pairings explicit.

69
00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.239
Practically, you should keep nesting to one or two levels deep.

70
00:04:56.959 --> 00:05:01.839
Deeper nesting becomes hard to read and maintain, and the clarity benefit disappears.

71
00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.320
When a section has internal structure that needs to be parsed separately,

72
00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.959
one level of nesting is usually all you need.

73
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.959
Separating data from instructions is one useful thing XML tags enable.

74
00:05:14.959 --> 00:05:17.359
The risk without it is prompt injection,

75
00:05:17.359 --> 00:05:21.440
where user-supplied content in the same undifferentiated block

76
00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.440
tries to override your task directive.

77
00:05:24.079 --> 00:05:29.519
By placing task directives inside an instructions tag and user-supplied content

78
00:05:29.519 --> 00:05:32.000
inside context or document tags,

79
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.000
you make it less likely the model confuses data for a directive.

80
00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:39.920
That said, tagging isn't a guaranteed barrier.

81
00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:43.119
Adversarial input can attempt to break out of a tag,

82
00:05:43.119 --> 00:05:46.880
so treat tags as one layer of clarity, not the primary defense.

83
00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.880
The real mitigations are treating any tool or document content as untrusted

84
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.799
and giving the model least privilege,

85
00:05:54.799 --> 00:05:58.320
so a compromised input can't trigger harmful actions.

86
00:06:00.079 --> 00:06:02.000
So here's the failure this prevents.

87
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.559
A customer email lands in the middle of your prompt

88
00:06:04.559 --> 00:06:06.880
with nothing marking where it starts,

89
00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.679
and the model treats a line inside it as a command.

90
00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.559
Tags close that gap because Claude is trained to honor that boundary

91
00:06:14.559 --> 00:06:17.519
in a way that it never honors a row of dashes.

92
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.480
They won't stop an injection on their own,

93
00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.320
but every real defense assumes that boundary is already in play.

94
00:06:25.119 --> 00:06:26.239
That's where we'll leave it.

95
00:06:26.239 --> 00:06:27.760
I'll see you in the next lesson.

